<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brielle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Den Briel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brielle is a small fortified town in South Holland near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Maas</a>, known in English as Brill. On 1 April 1572 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-beggars">Sea Beggars</a>, Calvinist privateers recently expelled from English ports, captured it from the Spanish, giving the rebels their first foothold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The capture set off revolts across Holland and Zeeland and is still commemorated in the town each year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brielle.jpg" alt="Brielle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Debot at Dutch Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brielle_vesting_20050518.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Brielle, called Den Briel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Brill in English, is a town and historic seaport in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, in the province of South Holland. It lies on the north side of the island of Voorne-Putten, at the mouth of the New Maas. Its name is derived from the Celtic word brogilo, meaning closed area or hunting grounds. The oldest writings indicate that the present town is the new Brielle, and that Den ouden Briel, or Old Brill, must have stood somewhere else on the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Brielle is a very old fortified town, which grew into a town of prestige in the 14th century. In 1330 Gerard van Voorne, independent Lord of Voorne and Viscount of Zeeland, granted it town rights. These gave it governmental and jurisdictional powers and trading rights, such as the right to trade fish and collect port taxes. It was for a long time the seat of the Lords of Voorne, until the fiefdom was added to the County of Holland in 1371. The town had its own harbour and traded with the countries around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, and even had a trading colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><p>In the Eighty Years' War the Capture of Brielle on 1 April 1572 by the Protestant Watergeuzen marked a turning point. Many towns in Holland then began to support William of Orange against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>, who had been sent to pacify the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The event is still marked each 1 April and on the night before, known as Chalk Night. After the capture the rebels tortured and murdered the Catholic Martyrs of Gorkum, and Brielle has since been a place of pilgrimage.</p><p>In August 1585 Brielle was one of three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> towns that became English possessions under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nonsuch">Treaty of Nonsuch</a>, when Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> received them as security for payment for 5,000 soldiers led by the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>. Its first English governor was Thomas Cecil, 1st Earl of Exeter, and in 1617 the towns returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former municipality covered 31.14 square kilometres and had 17,439 inhabitants in 2021. On 1 January 2023 it merged with Hellevoetsluis and Westvoorne into the new municipality of Voorne aan Zee. Brielle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> was named after the town by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonists of New Netherland. Maarten Tromp, the Dutch admiral, was born there in 1598.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brielle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William the Silent, Prince of Orange and stadtholder of Holland, Zeeland and Utrecht, led armed resistance from 1568, and in 1572 the capture of <b>Brielle</b> by the Sea Beggars allowed the rebels to take most towns of Holland and Zeeland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;Wilhelmus&quot; was written at the start of the Eighty Years' War, the Dutch Revolt against the Spanish Empire, at some time between April 1568 and the capture of <b>Brielle</b> on 1 April 1572, an event it does not mention.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch rebel privateers known as the Sea Beggars seized the port of <b>Brielle</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
